From 87288896d2925a0ca51d7e6243838c78d97ec6ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 07:24:50 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] [conversion] lower the vector dialect without region
 simplification

The pipeline ran MLIR's `convert-vector-to-llvm` on every module. That
pass applies its first pattern sets with the greedy driver's default
configuration, whose region simplification merges the similar blocks
of every function, also of one without any vector, and gives a merged
block one argument per operand that differs. In a large function with
many similar arms these arguments multiply from one merge to the next:
the pass took minutes and left blocks with hundreds of thousands of
arguments for LLVM. This is a cost of the MLIR pass, which has no
option for the driver's configuration; no LLVM report has been filed.

`reussir-convert-vector-to-llvm` applies the same pattern sets in the
same order, with that pass's default options and the same conversion
to the LLVM dialect, and with region simplification off. Folding stays
on, since the lowering patterns build on it. The pipeline and the JIT
bridge use it.

On the program that showed the cost, the lowering pipeline takes 2.7 s
where it took 536 s, and the build to the executable 8 s where it took
978 s. The merging also made code smaller. Without it the code of 53
programs of a compiler that targets Reussir is 2.0% larger in the
geometric mean (the largest 9.3%), with no measurable effect on run
time (geometric mean 1.000, 52 of 53 within 3%); the benchmark suite,
core and std do not change.
